-- ====================================================================================================================================
-- Hold Down Helper help page. Loaded on demand by OnLuaButton_InquiryHelp in Hold_Down_Helper.lua, the same way
-- Blum Drawer Maker loads its Help/*.xlua pages, and shown in an HTML_Dialog. ButtonOK is the dialog's Close button.
]] -- =====================================================]]
function HoldDownHelpHtml()
    return [==[<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><title>Hold Down Helper Help</title>
<style>
body { font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 13px; background-color: #FFFFFF; color: #000000; margin: 0; }
.layout { overflow: auto; height: 540px; padding: 4px 14px 4px 10px; }
h1 { font-size: 20px; margin: 10px 0 4px 0; }
h2 { font-size: 16px; margin: 16px 0 4px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #999999; }
p, li { line-height: 1.35; }
table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 4px 0; }
th, td { border: 1px solid #BBBBBB; padding: 3px 6px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
th { background-color: #EEEEEE; }
.warn { background-color: #FFF4CE; border: 1px solid #D9A400; padding: 6px 8px; margin: 8px 0; }
.danger { background-color: #FDE2E1; border: 1px solid #C0392B; padding: 6px 8px; margin: 8px 0; }
.foot { text-align: center; padding: 8px; border-top: 1px solid #999999; }
.FormButton { font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; }
</style></head>
<body>
<div class="layout">

<h1>Hold Down Helper</h1>
<p>Screwing a full sheet to the spoilboard means guessing where a fastener will not be hit by a cutter later.
This gadget finds positions on the <b>active sheet</b> that are clear of everything being cut, marks each one
with a small circle on a <b>Hold Down</b> layer, and creates one <b>Hold Down Dimples</b> drilling toolpath
that marks each position with a shallow V-bit dimple.</p>

<h2>The workflow</h2>
<ol>
<li>Draw and nest the job as usual, then run Hold Down Helper on the sheet you are about to cut.</li>
<li>Lay the sheet on the spoilboard and align it approximately.</li>
<li>Zero X and Y.</li>
<li>Run <b>only</b> the <b>Hold Down Dimples</b> toolpath, with the V-bit you chose.</li>
<li>Drive a screw at each dimple.</li>
<li>Load and run the real job toolpaths <b>without re-zeroing</b>.</li>
</ol>
<div class="danger"><b>Do not re-zero between steps 4 and 6.</b> The dimples are in job coordinates. Re-zeroing
X or Y moves every cut relative to the screws, and a cutter can then run straight into one.</div>

<h2>Before you run it</h2>
<ul>
<li>Have a <b>V-bit</b> in the tool database, defined in the <b>same units as the job</b>. The gadget refuses a
bit whose units do not match.</li>
<li>Show every layer that will be cut. <b>Hidden layers are not tested</b>. That is deliberate: it is how you
exclude part labels or construction lines. The completion message lists any hidden layers so a parts layer
hidden by accident is noticed.</li>
<li>Do not draw on the <b>Hold Down</b> layer. Re-running removes everything on it for the active sheet.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Settings</h2>
<p>All values are in the job's units. They are remembered between runs, separately for inch and metric jobs.
<b>Type each value and tab out of the field</b>. VCarve discards a pasted value.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Setting</th><th>Inch default</th><th>mm default</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td>V-Bit</td><td colspan="2">none</td><td>The bit that cuts the dimples. Only V-bits are offered. The
choice is remembered, as a snapshot: after editing the bit's feeds in the tool database, pick it again.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assumed tool diameter</td><td>0.25</td><td>6.0</td><td>The <b>largest</b> cutter used anywhere in the
job. The whole sheet is tested as if that cutter ran everywhere.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Screw head diameter</td><td>0.25</td><td>6.0</td><td>The diameter of the fastener head.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Margin</td><td>0.125</td><td>3.0</td><td>Extra clearance on top of the cutter and the head.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Edge inset</td><td>1.25</td><td>32.0</td><td>How far in from the sheet edge the perimeter screws
sit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Perimeter spacing target</td><td>16.0</td><td>406.4</td><td>The spacing aimed for along each edge. Each
side gets at least 2 screws; a 48" side gets 3.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Field count</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>Screws in the middle of the sheet, up to 36. Zero places
perimeter screws only.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Max nudge search</td><td>3.0</td><td>76.2</td><td>How far a rejected position may move looking for a
safe spot.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dimple depth</td><td>0.1</td><td>2.5</td><td>How deep the V-bit cuts. Must be less than the sheet
thickness. A 90 degree bit at 0.1" leaves a dimple about 0.2" wide.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marker diameter</td><td>0.125</td><td>3.0</td><td>The size of the circle drawn at each position. It
only gives the drilling toolpath something to drill at; the V-bit drills at its center.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Safe Z gap</td><td>0.25</td><td>6.0</td><td>The height above the sheet for moves between dimples, up to
1" (25.4 mm). The sheet is not fastened yet, so it may bow or rock; raise this if the bit drags.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>How a position is judged safe</h2>
<p>A position is rejected if it is <b>inside</b> any closed vector (never through a part), or closer than
<b>R</b> to any vector, where</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;"><b>R = assumed tool diameter + half the screw head diameter + margin</b>
&nbsp;(0.5" at the inch defaults)</p>
<p>The <b>full</b> tool diameter is used, not half of it, because an outside profile runs the cutter beside the
vector: its far edge reaches a whole diameter beyond the line.</p>
<p>Positions must also keep half a screw head away from the sheet edge.</p>

<h2>Where it tries to put screws</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Perimeter:</b> evenly spaced along each edge at the edge inset, never in a corner.</li>
<li><b>Field:</b> a grid in the middle half of the sheet. 4 makes a 2 x 2 grid, 6 a 2 x 3.</li>
<li><b>Nudging:</b> a rejected <b>perimeter</b> position slides along its own edge, a step at a time in either
direction, and <b>never moves inward</b>: a screw that wandered into the field would no longer hold the edge
down. A rejected <b>field</b> position searches outward in rings.</li>
<li>A position with nowhere safe within the max nudge search is <b>reported, not placed</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Reading the result</h2>
<ul>
<li>The message states the <b>R</b> in use. If far more positions are rejected than you expect, check R first.
A test value left in the dialog, such as a large margin, is remembered and quietly shrinks the usable area.</li>
<li>Each position it could not place is listed with the <b>layer and extent of the vector that blocked it</b>,
or "too close to the sheet edge".</li>
<li>A <b>NOTE</b> lists objects with no vector outline, such as text that has not been converted to curves.
Positions are kept clear of each one's bounding box instead. Anything marked <b>NOT TESTED</b> could not even
be bounded and was ignored, so look at those areas yourself.</li>
<li>If every position is rejected, nothing is drawn and nothing is deleted. Markers and a toolpath from an
earlier run stay in place and may no longer be safe: delete them before cutting.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Re-running</h2>
<p>Each run that places at least one position removes the active sheet's markers and its Hold Down Dimples
toolpath, then builds new ones, so changed settings replace the old dimples rather than adding to them.
Pressing Cancel changes nothing. Other sheets are never touched. Dimples already cut into a sheet stay there, of
course: drive screws only at the dimples from the latest run.</p>

<h2>Gotchas</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Edge inset versus your nesting margin.</b> If parts are nested close to the sheet edge, the perimeter
positions land on or near them and are rejected all along that edge. Lower the edge inset so the screws sit in
the strip between the parts and the sheet edge. With the defaults, that strip must be wider than the inset plus
R.</li>
<li><b>Waste islands are not detected.</b> Clearance from every vector does not prove the material under a
screw stays attached to the sheet. A region fully enclosed by cuts comes free during the job, and a screw in it
releases a loose piece under a spinning cutter. <b>Look at the positions before you drill.</b></li>
<li><b>One tool diameter for the whole sheet.</b> A job mixing a 1/8" and a 1/4" bit is tested as though the
1/4" ran everywhere. That costs usable area but never errs toward danger.</li>
<li><b>Toolpaths are not read.</b> The test uses vectors. A vector with no toolpath still blocks placement, and
a toolpath whose vector was deleted does not.</li>
<li><b>Toolpath extras are not modeled.</b> Lead-ins, ramps, overcuts and machining allowances can reach beyond
R. Raise the margin to cover them.</li>
<li><b>Active sheet only.</b> Run it once per sheet, on the sheet you are about to cut.</li>
<li><b>Small sheets.</b> The gadget is meant for full sheets and warns below 24 x 24 inches (610 x 610 mm), but
carries on.</li>
</ul>

</div>
<div class="foot">
<input id="ButtonOK" class="FormButton" name="ButtonOK" type="button" value="Close">
</div>
</body></html>]==]
end
-- =============== End of File =========================]]
